[38;2;124;124;124mpart, sorted by ascending imaginary part?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. The non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function are [0m[38;2;124;124;124mknown to be infinite in number. They lie in the critical strip 0 < Re(s) < 1. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe number of zeros with imaginary part between 0 and T is given by the Riemann-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mvon Mangoldt formula:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mN(T) = (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) + O(log T)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAs T → ∞, N(T) → ∞. So there are infinitely many non-trivial zeros with positive[0m[38;2;124;124;124mimaginary part.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince there are infinitely many zeros sorted by ascending imaginary part, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mIm(z_n) is a strictly increasing sequence that goes to infinity.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe limit of Im(z_n) as n → ∞ is +∞.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is straightforward from the fact that there are infinitely many non-trivial[0m[38;2;124;124;124mzeros and they extend to arbitrarily large imaginary parts.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write a clean proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. The Riemann zeta function ζ(s) has non-trivial zeros only in the critical [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstrip 0 < Re(s) < 1 (this is a classical result).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The Riemann-von Mangoldt counting formula: N(T) = #{ρ = β + iγ : ζ(ρ) = 0, 0 [0m[38;2;124;124;124m< γ ≤ T} = (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) + O(log T).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. As T → ∞, N(T) → ∞, so there are infinitely many non-trivial zeros with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpositive imaginary part.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. The zeros are discrete (no accumulation point in the finite plane, since ζ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmeromorphic with only a pole at s=1).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. Since there are infinitely many zeros with positive imaginary part and they [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdon't accumulate at any finite point, sorting them by ascending imaginary part [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgives a sequence z_n with Im(z_n) → ∞.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. The zeros could in principle have bounded [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mimaginary part but accumulate. But ζ(s) is meromorphic (holomorphic except for a[0m[38;2;124;124;124msimple pole at s=1), so its zeros are isolated — they cannot accumulate at any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinite point. Therefore, in any bounded region, there are only finitely many [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mzeros. Combined with the fact that there are infinitely many zeros with positive[0m[38;2;124;124;124mimaginary part (from N(T) → ∞), the sorted sequence must have Im(z_n) → ∞.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is +∞.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof.[0m

